    hard: [
      { q: 'Explain the trolley problem and your own position on it.', a: 'A runaway trolley will kill 5 people; you can pull a lever to divert it, killing 1. It tests utilitarianism (save more) vs. deontology (never use someone as a means). There\'s no single right answer! 🚃' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between inductive and deductive reasoning?', a: 'Deductive: starts with a general rule to reach a certain conclusion. Inductive: starts with specific observations to form a probable general conclusion! 🧠' },
      { q: 'What caused the 2008 financial crisis?', a: 'Risky mortgage lending in the USA, bundled into complex financial products, collapsed when housing prices fell — triggering a global banking meltdown! 💸' },
      { q: 'What is the anthropic principle in cosmology?', a: 'The idea that the universe\'s fundamental constants must be compatible with conscious life existing to observe them — we exist, therefore conditions must allow our existence! 🌌' },
      { q: 'Explain Occam\'s Razor and give an example of it in everyday thinking.', a: 'The simplest explanation that fits the facts is usually correct. E.g., if your car won\'t start, assume a flat battery before suspecting sabotage! 🪒' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between correlation and causation? Why does it matter?', a: 'Correlation: two things vary together. Causation: one thing actually causes the other. Ice cream sales and drowning rates both rise in summer — but ice cream doesn\'t cause drowning! 📊' },
      { q: 'What is the philosophical argument of Plato\'s Allegory of the Cave?', a: 'Prisoners chained in a cave see only shadows on a wall and mistake them for reality. The philosopher escapes and sees the real world — a metaphor for education and true knowledge! 🏛️' },
      { q: 'How does mRNA vaccine technology work (as introduced with COVID vaccines)?', a: 'mRNA vaccines deliver genetic instructions for cells to produce a harmless protein (e.g., the COVID spike). The immune system learns to recognise and fight it — no live virus needed! 💉' },
      { q: 'What is the significance of the Turing Test in the history of AI?', a: 'Proposed by Alan Turing in 1950 — if a machine can converse indistinguishably from a human, it can be called intelligent. It set the first benchmark for AI capability! 🤖' },
      { q: 'What is the paradox of tolerance and why is it controversial?', a: 'Karl Popper\'s argument: a tolerant society must be intolerant of intolerance, or intolerance will destroy tolerance itself. Controversial because it can justify suppressing speech! ⚖️' },
      { q: 'What is entropy and why does it always increase?', a: 'Entropy is disorder or randomness in a system. The Second Law of Thermodynamics says closed systems naturally move toward greater disorder — because there are far more disordered states than ordered ones! 🌀' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between socialism, communism, and capitalism?', a: 'Capitalism: private ownership, free market. Socialism: collective/state ownership with some markets. Communism: full collective ownership, no private property, classless society! 🔄' },
      { q: 'What is the prisoner\'s dilemma and what does it reveal about cooperation?', a: 'Two suspects choose to cooperate (stay silent) or betray. If both betray, both lose. It shows that individual rational choices can lead to collectively worse outcomes! 🤝' },
      { q: 'Explain what dark matter is and why it is theorised to exist.', a: 'Invisible matter that doesn\'t emit light but has gravitational effects — galaxies rotate too fast to be explained by visible matter alone, so dark matter must exist! 🌌' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between a law and a theory in science?', a: 'A scientific law describes what happens (e.g., gravity pulls); a theory explains why (e.g., general relativity). Theories are not "just guesses" — they are rigorously tested! 🔬' },
      { q: 'What is confirmation bias and how does it affect judgement?', a: 'The tendency to seek, interpret, and remember information that confirms existing beliefs — making people less likely to update their views with contradictory evidence! 🧠' },
      { q: 'Explain the concept of opportunity cost in economics.', a: 'The value of the best alternative you give up when making a choice. E.g., using land for a car park has an opportunity cost of whatever else could be built there! 💰' },
      { q: 'What is the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle?', a: 'It is impossible to know both the exact position and exact momentum of a particle simultaneously — the more precisely you know one, the less precisely you can know the other! ⚛️' },
      { q: 'What is the significance of the Enlightenment in European history?', a: 'The 17th–18th century movement that promoted reason, science, individual rights, and religious tolerance — laying foundations for democracy and the American/French revolutions! 💡' },
      { q: 'What is cognitive dissonance?', a: 'The mental discomfort of holding two contradictory beliefs at once — people reduce it by changing one belief, ignoring the conflict, or rationalising! 🧠' },
      { q: 'Explain the concept of moral relativism.', a: 'The view that moral judgements are not universally true — what\'s right or wrong depends on cultural, historical, or personal context rather than absolute standards! ⚖️' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between empathy and sympathy?', a: 'Empathy is feeling what another person feels (perspective-taking); sympathy is feeling for someone without necessarily sharing their emotional state! 💖' },
      { q: 'What is the butterfly effect and how does it apply to real life?', a: 'In chaos theory, tiny changes (a butterfly flapping wings) can lead to vastly different outcomes later (a hurricane). Real example: a missed meeting can change the course of a career! 🦋' },
      { q: 'What is the tragedy of the commons?', a: 'When individuals acting in self-interest deplete a shared resource — e.g., overfishing, deforestation. No individual has incentive to conserve what everyone shares! 🌊' },
      { q: 'How do solar panels convert sunlight into electricity?', a: 'Photovoltaic cells use the photoelectric effect — photons knock electrons loose from silicon, generating a current. Multiple cells form a solar panel! ☀️' },
    ],
